---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Icon from '../components/Icon.astro';
const formats = [
  [
    'EMOM',
    'Every minute on the minute',
    'Your strength & easy days',
    'Start the listed movement at the top of each minute. Complete the reps with control, then rest for the balance. The next minute is the next movement.',
    'Minute 1: 4–5 left presses. Minute 2: 4–5 right presses. Aim to finish work by about :40. If you cannot, lower the reps.',
  ],
  [
    'E2MOM',
    'Every two minutes on the minute',
    'Your athletic day',
    'Start a station every two minutes. The longer window gives you time to change sides, finish quality reps, and recover.',
    '0:00 clean & jerk; 2:00 snatch; 4:00 mixed complex. Finish each station by about 1:30, then rest at least 30 seconds.',
  ],
  [
    'Complex',
    'A sequence of movements',
    'Your athletic day',
    'Perform the prescribed movements in order. Some complexes are unbroken; in this plan you may park the bell and reset between movements.',
    '10 swings → 6 goblet squats → 6 rows per side. Keep this inside the two-minute station. Reduce reps if you lose the rest.',
  ],
  [
    'AMRAP',
    'As many rounds as possible',
    'Format reference',
    'Repeat a fixed sequence for a set time. For sustainable training, choose a quality pace and take rest as needed; the clock is a limit, not a reason to rush.',
    'Example only: 8 minutes alternating 5 deadlifts + 5 rows per side. Track smooth rounds. AMRAP is not prescribed in this block.',
  ],
  [
    'For time',
    'Fixed work, measured time',
    'Format reference',
    'Complete a prescribed amount of work and note how long it takes. A time cap limits the session. Technique and appropriate effort still come first.',
    'Example only: 3 rounds of 8 deadlifts + 6 rows per side, with an 8-minute cap. Stop at the cap. This block has no for-time tests.',
  ],
  [
    'Straight sets',
    'Work, then recover',
    'Format reference',
    'Complete a set, rest for a chosen duration, then repeat. Useful when technique or a heavier load needs more recovery than an EMOM allows.',
    'Example only: 3 sets of 5 strict presses per side, resting 60–90 seconds between sets. Use controlled reps; avoid failure.',
  ],
];
const replacements = [
  [
    'Knee sensitive during squats',
    'Chair squat',
    '6–10 bodyweight reps to a stable chair at comfortable depth. Keep the same minute.',
  ],
  [
    'Knee sensitive during jerk dip',
    'Strict press',
    '3 reps per side if comfortable, in the same two-minute station.',
  ],
  [
    'Snatch catch or grip deteriorates',
    'Single-arm clean',
    '4 reps per side, then recover for the balance of the two minutes.',
  ],
  [
    'Overhead motion uncomfortable',
    'Floor press / deadlift',
    'For strict press, use a comfortable floor press. For the snatch station, use 8–10 deadlifts. Stop movements that hurt.',
  ],
  [
    'Swing rhythm or back fatigue',
    'Two-handed deadlift',
    '8–10 controlled reps in the same minute. Do not push through back pain.',
  ],
  [
    'Floor setup unfamiliar',
    'Push-ups + rest',
    'On Day 3, do 6–15 push-ups in minute 3, then rest for minute 4. Elevate hands on a secure surface if needed.',
  ],
  [
    'Kickstand balance interferes',
    'Bilateral RDL',
    '8 reps in each scheduled side minute; you may rest the second minute to reduce work.',
  ],
  [
    'Small room or knee dislikes marching',
    'Suitcase hold',
    'Stay still: 20 seconds per side, park to change hands, then rest.',
  ],
];
---

<Layout title="Training guide" active="guide">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="page-intro">
      <div class="eyebrow rust">
        <span class="dash"></span> UNDERSTAND YOUR TRAINING
      </div>
      <h1>
        A little clarity.
        <br />A lot more confidence.
      </h1>
      <p>
        Know what the clock means, how to adapt a movement, and when to
        progress. Your session should fit your recovery as well as your
        schedule.
      </p>
    </div>
    <section class="guide-section" style="padding-top:0">
      <div class="section-top">
        <div>
          <div class="eyebrow">THE FORMATS, EXPLAINED</div>
          <h2>Work. Rest. Repeat.</h2>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="formats-grid">
        {formats.map(([name, full, use, body, example], i) => (
          <article class:list={['format-card', { 'active-format': i < 3 }]}>
            <span class:list={['pill', { olive: i < 3 }]}>{use}</span>
            <h3>{name}</h3>
            <div class="eyebrow" style="margin-bottom:12px">
              {full}
            </div>
            <p>{body}</p>
            <div class="format-example">
              <strong>
                {i < 3 ? 'In your program' : 'Illustrative example'}
              </strong>
              {example}
            </div>
          </article>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="guide-section" id="progression">
      <h2>Progress when you are ready.</h2>
      <p>
        Effort means perceived exertion on a 1–10 scale: 5–6 feels comfortable
        to moderate; 7–8 is challenging but controlled; 10 is maximal. Reps in
        reserve means how many more good repetitions you could do before
        failure. Your strength work should usually finish with 2–3 left.
      </p>
      <p>
        Week 1 establishes your baseline. In Week 2, Tuesday gains a round; try
        the displayed rep increase on just one strength lift if recovery is
        good. Week 3 offers small optional increases. You can keep the starting
        reps on any station, even when the website displays a higher target.
        Week 4 reduces the work to four 20-minute sessions.
      </p>
      <div class="cue-box">
        Advance only when the reps are controlled, effort is within the day’s
        range, and the knee feels normal during training and the next morning.
        Keep 2–3 strength reps in reserve. If you need more rest, do fewer reps
        or repeat a week.
      </div>
      <p>
        The 20-minute toggle reduces the rounds and accessory work while
        preserving a warm-up and recovery. Keep Monday and Thursday as strength
        days, Tuesday easier, and Saturday skill-focused. Wednesday, Friday, and
        Sunday are recovery days; comfortable walking is welcome.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section class="guide-section" id="replacements">
      <h2>A useful swap keeps the purpose.</h2>
      <p>
        Choose the alternative that suits the issue today. Keep the interval and
        use the suggested dose; a replacement does not have to match the
        original exercise rep for rep.
      </p>
      <div class="replacement-list">
        {replacements.map(([when, swap, dose]) => (
          <div class="replacement-item">
            <strong>{when}</strong>
            <strong class="arrow">→ {swap}</strong>
            <p>{dose}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="guide-section" id="recovery">
      <h2>Train for the next session, too.</h2>
      <div class="recovery-grid">
        <article class="recovery-card">
          <Icon name="heart" />
          <h3>Listen to the knee.</h3>
          <p>
            You are currently pain-free. Keep it that way: use comfortable depth
            and smooth reps. If pain appears or increases, stop that movement
            and try a comfortable alternative. New swelling, locking, giving
            way, or symptoms that persist into the next day call for a clinician
            or physiotherapist review.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="recovery-card">
          <Icon name="strength" />
          <h3>Protect your muscle.</h3>
          <p>
            Keep the strength sessions consistent and include protein across
            meals. A practical range to discuss with your clinician or dietitian
            is about 100–130 g/day, adjusted for appetite, overall intake, and
            kidney health. Avoid aggressive calorie cuts; your recent gradual
            weight loss is a reason to stay steady.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="recovery-card">
          <Icon name="wave" />
          <h3>Keep the easy work easy.</h3>
          <p>
            Add comfortable walks as recovery allows. Hydrate and adjust
            training if semaglutide causes nausea, vomiting, dizziness, or low
            intake. New unexplained muscle pain or weakness while taking
            rosuvastatin deserves medical advice; severe symptoms or dark urine
            need urgent assessment.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:20px">
        This is a training plan, not treatment for the knee or fatty liver.
        Follow any exercise restrictions from your treating clinician and keep
        your medication and follow-up plan with them.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section class="guide-section bottom-space" id="sources">
      <h2>Sources & credits.</h2>
      <p>
        An original plan informed by your training experience and preferences.
        It is inspired by Lebe Stark’s accessible kettlebell approach and is not
        an official Lebe Stark program or an endorsement.
      </p>
      <ul class="sources">
        <li>
          <a href="https://en.lebestark.ch/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">
            Lebe Stark
          </a>{' '}
          — inspiration and linked movement tutorials. Each video names its
          creator and links to the original.
        </li>
        <li>
          Additional demonstrations:{' '}
          <a href="https://www.e3rehab.com/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">
            E3 Rehab
          </a>
          ,{' '}
          <a
            href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ztXjknHC9NQ"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noreferrer"
          >
            Kathletics
          </a>
          ,{' '}
          <a
            href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yXstm050X84"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noreferrer"
          >
            NASM
          </a>
          , and{' '}
          <a
            href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=N6Rvg3EH8mM"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noreferrer"
          >
            Zack Henderson
          </a>
          .
        </li>
        <li>
          <a
            href="https://doi.org/10.1136/bjsports-2024-108110"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noreferrer"
          >
            Best practice guide for patellofemoral pain
          </a>{' '}
          — education and individualized exercise.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a
            href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12125019/"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noreferrer"
          >
            Joint advisory on nutritional priorities during GLP-1 therapy
          </a>{' '}
          — nutrition and muscle preservation.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a
            href="https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00125-024-06196-3"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noreferrer"
          >
            EASL–EASD–EASO MASLD guidance
          </a>{' '}
          — regular physical activity and resistance exercise.
        </li>
        <li>
          Hero photograph via{' '}
          <a href="https://unsplash.com/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">
            Unsplash
          </a>
          . Demonstration videos and images remain with their creators.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="/downloads/program.pdf" download>
            Download your original printable four-week plan
          </a>
          .
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Videos load from YouTube or Vimeo only when you press play. Thumbnails
        and the hero photograph load from external image services. Your session
        journal is stored in this browser, with an export option.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</Layout>
